  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA weakening mesoscale convective complex driven by an apparent\nMCV continues to track across the forecast area in Ohio. Here in\nthis region, the 2.0 to 2.2 PWATs have come to fruition and\nrainfall rates out west show some 1.5 to 2.0 rainfall rates. The\nshort term hi res models show some uptick in rainfall rates as\nit tracks east into the the WV panhandle and into Allegheny\nCounty. \n\nHere, the HREF hints at 1.00 to 1.70 rainfall amounts from\nmidnight through 10am moving east into the WV panhandle and the\nPA border and eventually into Allegheny county. This is of \ncourse only the mean values and higher values are certainly \npossible and will thus keep the Flood Watch going. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nConfidence in what follows for Sun and Sun night drops off, as \natmospheric recovery/Sun-afternoon storm evolution will depend \non the mesoscale setup left behind by the overnight into Sunday\nmorning complex. While stable morning air should limit coverage\nand intensity of convection at least into early afternoon, \nredevelopment appears increasingly likely tied to a potential \nmesoscale convective vortex developing from storms in the \nMidwest/northern Plains. The ensemble models such as the LREF\nsuggest a second 500MB shortwave tracking across the IN and OH\nregion and into the forecast area. This may be the one needed\ntrigger to help the recovery form the morning convection.  \n\nHere, a pocket of 25-30 knots of 0-6km shear could provide \nenough storm organization for a damaging wind potential, while \nelongated/curved low-level hodographs (particularly enhanced by\na MCV if present) and sufficient 0-1km SRH suggest a lower-end \ntornado threat for esewd-moving storms. Although the interesting\nfactor here is that the CAMs currently do not produce an\norganized MCS and rather a cluster of storms developing. \nMachine- learning guidance does support these assertions, likely\npicking up on MCV- enhanced fields. Also, the same favorable \nheavy- rainfall ingredients from the previous night will \ncontinue, extending the flash flood threat into Sun night.\nAnother night of PWATs between 2.0 and 2.2 will keep a threat of\nflooding across the area. Thus, the Flood Watch will continue. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nDiurnal stabilization should begin to limit convective coverage\nand intensity late Sun evening, with a few showers lingering \ninto Mon with one last shortwave trough. By this point, PWAT \nwill be dropping off thanks to developing drier nwly flow, \nwhich should help to taper the heavy-rainfall threat. High \npressure building in from the Great Lakes should end \nprecipitation by Mon evening. Dry weather is then favored until\nWed, when the next front could potentially arrive, although \nthis timing could change.",
